//
// What the offline queue survives, and what loses it. Two fields per store and the difference
// between them matters: survives/lostTo are what is true of the store, and says is what a screen
// renders. A store whose behaviour is wider than what it says is a screen with a gap in it.
//
// Nothing here is encrypted by MyThuso and nothing here is sent anywhere. Fictional readings.

package za.co.mythuso.model

/**
 * Something that can happen to a phone or a tab, named once so two platforms cannot call the same
 * event by two names and then disagree about it without anybody noticing.
 */
data class DurabilityEvent(val id: String, val name: String)

/**
 * One place work waits. survives and lostTo are behaviour; saysSurvives and saysLostTo are the
 * sentences a screen renders, and they are deliberately separate fields.
 */
data class QueueStore(
    val id: String,
    val platform: String,
    /** "file" or "memory". A memory store is one that says out loud that it keeps nothing. */
    val kind: String,
    val heldIn: String,
    val survives: List<String>,
    val lostTo: List<String>,
    val saysSurvives: List<String>,
    val saysLostTo: List<String>
)

/**
 * What is still true after the disk has refused the work. Named once, so that two platforms cannot
 * promise the same thing in words a check cannot compare.
 */
data class WriteOutcome(val id: String, val name: String)

/**
 * A way the disk refuses. leaves is behaviour, in outcome ids; says is the one sentence a screen
 * renders; saidBy is which stores render it today, and it is deliberately not every store that can
 * suffer it.
 */
data class WriteFailure(
    val id: String,
    val name: String,
    val detectedBy: String,
    val leaves: List<String>,
    val says: String,
    val saidBy: List<String>
)

/* What the offline queue survives, and what loses it — as data, per platform, because it was
   prose twice about the same disk. Android's FileBook carried three sentences about a JSON file
   in the app's private storage; iOS's CaptureQueueView and VisitQueueView carried six about a
   JSON file in Application Support; the web's queue screen carried one about a module-level
   array. A nurse reading the Android sentence and the iOS list would not have known they
   describe one behaviour, and nothing in this repository compared them. This section is that
   comparison. It does not flatten three platforms into one sentence: the web genuinely keeps
   less, because scripts/check-boundaries.mjs forbids the browser's local storage, session
   storage and IndexedDB across apps/web/src and a preview must not leave a patient's readings on
   a borrowed machine. What it does is make the promise one promise and the differences visible. */
val durabilityEvents = listOf(
    DurabilityEvent("navigation", "Moving between screens"),
    DurabilityEvent("reload", "Reloading the page or closing the tab"),
    DurabilityEvent("close-app", "Closing the app"),
    DurabilityEvent("crash", "A crash, a force-quit, or the process being killed"),
    DurabilityEvent("restart", "Restarting the phone"),
    DurabilityEvent("sign-out", "Signing out"),
    DurabilityEvent("delete", "Deleting the app"),
    DurabilityEvent("clear-storage", "Clearing the app's storage from the operating system's settings"),
    DurabilityEvent("factory-reset", "A factory reset"),
    DurabilityEvent("lost-phone", "Losing the phone"),
    DurabilityEvent("second-device", "Picking up a second device"),
)

